959
01:07:43,682 --> 01:07:47,640
NTSB is up my ass with a flashlight,
we had to give them access to everything.

960
01:07:47,943 --> 01:07:49,991
Everbody's curious about Captain Whitaker.

961
01:07:50,034 --> 01:07:53,203
Well I flew with him in the Navy
and he is a great pilot.

962
01:07:53,430 --> 01:07:54,550
Huh.

963
01:07:55,792 --> 01:07:57,188
Is he a drunk?

964
01:08:01,217 --> 01:08:03,879
He's a heavy drinker.
But maybe I should bring in him?

965
01:08:03,903 --> 01:08:05,303
In a minute.

966
01:08:05,956 --> 01:08:08,395
Alright, so let’s get down to it, Lenny.

967
01:08:08,823 --> 01:08:12,555
What does the union plan to do
about this blood test saying

968
01:08:12,792 --> 01:08:15,414
Whitaker was high on booze and coke?

969
01:08:15,721 --> 01:08:17,203
This guy’s a real peach.

970
01:08:17,419 --> 01:08:19,596
I'm gonna kill the toxicology report.

971
01:08:20,660 --> 01:08:23,562
The testing was done incompetently.

972
01:08:23,580 --> 01:08:28,643
The toxicology equipment they used
was last calibrated in June of 2009.

973
01:08:28,793 --> 01:08:31,373
It's already 18 months past code.

974
01:08:31,647 --> 01:08:35,770
Their log that should clearly state
who labeled the blood vials and when,

975
01:08:35,787 --> 01:08:39,730
is very incomplete, and they used a
preservative in the blood

976
01:08:39,803 --> 01:08:42,542
that has been known to cause fermentation,

977
01:08:42,782 --> 01:08:45,860
and in some cases register
higher in an alcohol test.

978
01:08:45,870 --> 01:08:49,503
Now, I have done all this, I've
only been here a few days. So...

979
01:08:49,560 --> 01:08:51,777
I wouldn't worry about it too much,
I can handle this.

980
01:08:52,274 --> 01:08:53,696
I like this guy, Lenny.

981
01:08:53,907 --> 01:08:56,891
Makes me wanna go out
sniff a few lines and fly a jet.

982
01:08:57,283 --> 01:08:58,399
Mr. Carr,

983
01:08:58,400 --> 01:09:02,374
we’re gonna fight to push all the fault
on the manufacturer, Jackson Ridgefield.

984
01:09:02,520 --> 01:09:05,986
And you think they’re gonna just open
their check book and buy us all lunch?

985
01:09:06,330 --> 01:09:08,016
Good fucking luck.

986
01:09:08,709 --> 01:09:10,179
What’s my exposure Jim?

987
01:09:11,555 --> 01:09:14,248
Well, the awards to the
families could kill the airline.

988
01:09:14,270 --> 01:09:15,408
Fuck the airline.

989
01:09:15,430 --> 01:09:17,530
The insurance companies
can have the goddamm airline.

990
01:09:17,624 --> 01:09:19,764
I just don’t want them come
sniffing in my other pockets.

991
01:09:19,789 --> 01:09:24,028
Speaking for the pilots’ union, sir,
we don’t want to see your airline go away.

992
01:09:24,029 --> 01:09:25,936
We enjoy the contract you have with us.

993
01:09:26,281 --> 01:09:29,669
We gonna protect Captain Whitaker,
and in turn we gonna protect your airline.

994
01:09:30,691 --> 01:09:34,304
Does... Whitaker know he's goin' to jail?

995
01:09:34,974 --> 01:09:37,044
My client isn't goin' to jail, Mr. Carr.

996
01:09:37,083 --> 01:09:39,883
Oh, he's goin' to jail.
He belongs in jail.

997
01:09:39,993 --> 01:09:42,733
Bet your ass he's goin' to jail,
the only question is...

998
01:09:42,789 --> 01:09:44,823
is he gonna die in jail?

999
01:09:44,824 --> 01:09:46,857
You are wrong, Mr. Carr.

1000
01:09:47,220 --> 01:09:50,442
Last time I checked, six counts
of manslaughter, it's life in prison.

1001
01:09:51,170 --> 01:09:52,761
Life in prison...

1002
01:09:53,199 --> 01:09:56,417
What we in Georgia call “all day long”.

1003
01:10:04,351 --> 01:10:06,583
I hear you're some kind o' pilot.

1004
01:10:33,335 --> 01:10:36,192
Theeere she is, there she is.

1005
01:10:36,223 --> 01:10:40,254
Back to the meet?
Had a nice meeting?

1006
01:10:43,268 --> 01:10:45,654
I know, it's okay, uh?

1007
01:10:49,258 --> 01:10:50,658
It's okay...

1008
01:10:51,425 --> 01:10:53,307
Yup, that's it.

1009
01:10:59,764 --> 01:11:00,884
Me?

1010
01:11:02,928 --> 01:11:04,328
That's it.

1011
01:11:05,449 --> 01:11:07,847
Am I-No.
Yeah, no.

1012
01:11:17,727 --> 01:11:20,646
<i>You wanna run the boy, you wanna be
a quarterback, let me see you run it back!</i>

1013
01:11:20,920 --> 01:11:23,451
<i>So say "Dowdow", say "Kwakwa"!</i>

1014
01:12:54,057 --> 01:12:56,519
– Good morning.
– Hello.

1015
01:13:01,549 --> 01:13:03,804
You were in really
bad shape last night.

1016
01:13:03,890 --> 01:13:07,007
Yeah, true...
just tired.

1017
01:13:09,726 --> 01:13:12,387
Drank a little bit too much.

1018
01:13:12,607 --> 01:13:13,727
A bit?

1019
01:13:15,315 --> 01:13:18,008
What, you won't count the fucking beers?

1020
01:13:18,177 --> 01:13:19,685
I threw ‘em out.

1021
01:13:20,787 --> 01:13:22,612
They're in the garbage, vodka too.

1022
01:13:24,592 --> 01:13:26,171
Is that okay with you?

1023
01:13:26,415 --> 01:13:27,635
More than okay.

1024
01:13:32,619 --> 01:13:35,492
I’ve been watching the sky
change as the sun rises.

1025
01:13:37,590 --> 01:13:39,173
Beautiful.

1026
01:13:40,040 --> 01:13:42,953
I went out there, I took some great shots.

1027
01:13:47,716 --> 01:13:50,470
It's just so beautiful here.

1028
01:13:51,284 --> 01:13:52,915
You grew up here?

1029
01:13:53,149 --> 01:13:55,264
No, Dad did.

1030
01:13:58,279 --> 01:14:00,448
It's my grandfather's farm.

1031
01:14:03,318 --> 01:14:04,347
Was.

1032
01:14:06,136 --> 01:14:10,626
Dad was a pilot, he was a...
332nd airbone. He was a...

1033
01:14:10,876 --> 01:14:13,524
Tuskee, he built that
landing strip out there.

1034
01:14:15,209 --> 01:14:20,269
Crop dustin'. EPA shut him down, he...
traded the crop duster for...

1035
01:14:21,007 --> 01:14:24,372
that old Cesna sittin'
out there in the barn.

1036
01:14:26,523 --> 01:14:30,184
He flew that damn thing
all over the place.

1037
01:14:32,866 --> 01:14:36,481
He died... I got divorced, and...

1038
01:14:39,198 --> 01:14:41,711
been trying to sell the place ever since.

1039
01:14:44,098 --> 01:14:45,869
What about your mom?

1040
01:14:46,378 --> 01:14:47,705
Dead.

1041
01:14:50,323 --> 01:14:51,938
You? What about your Dad?

1042
01:14:52,717 --> 01:14:55,519
Well, I'm pretty sure he...
lives in Colorado.

1043
01:14:55,668 --> 01:14:56,788
Yeah?

1044
01:14:58,056 --> 01:14:59,530
No relationship?

1045
01:15:00,403 --> 01:15:02,514
It was my mom that raised me.

1046
01:15:02,515 --> 01:15:04,042
She was incredible.

1047
01:15:05,515 --> 01:15:09,169
She held it together for so long,
I couldn’t tell how sick she was.

1048
01:15:11,800 --> 01:15:15,142
We spent those last seven months
together like we were teenagers.

1049
01:15:17,487 --> 01:15:21,630
We traded stories about
boys and... whatever.

1050
01:15:24,923 --> 01:15:26,804
Oh, she loved me.

1051
01:15:44,221 --> 01:15:45,867
My name's Trevor.

1052
01:15:46,359 --> 01:15:48,681
You saved my mom.

1053
01:16:00,802 --> 01:16:02,271
– It's great to see you, Whip.

1054
01:16:02,272 --> 01:16:04,143
– It's great to see you, Whip.
– It's good to see you too.

1055
01:16:10,269 --> 01:16:12,009
The service was so nice.

1056
01:16:12,119 --> 01:16:14,815
I mean, all those people
who wanted to say goodbye.

1057
01:16:16,856 --> 01:16:20,041
And Trina looked so beautiful in there.
Don't you think you?

1058
01:16:20,738 --> 01:16:24,384
I mean, like she was gonna...
wake up and smile!

1059
01:16:24,688 --> 01:16:28,076
I didn’t go in, Margaret.
I... I just couldn’t.

1060
01:16:29,499 --> 01:16:30,590
Trevor...

1061
01:16:31,293 --> 01:16:34,496
Find daddy, baby.
He's over there with Mr. Benton.

1062
01:16:41,552 --> 01:16:43,830
You really should come
to the counseling group.

1063
01:16:43,988 --> 01:16:46,134
The Union has great
people for us to talk to.

1064
01:16:46,160 --> 01:16:48,493
I can't.
Really I...

1065
01:16:49,525 --> 01:16:50,645
I will.

1066
01:16:54,045 --> 01:16:56,076
– Yeah. Well...
– Margaret.

1067
01:16:57,866 --> 01:16:58,968
– You take care, okay, Whip?

1068
01:16:58,969 --> 01:16:59,840
– You take care, okay, Whip?
– Okay.

1069
01:17:00,496 --> 01:17:02,684
Listen, Margaret, I have a...

1070
01:17:04,595 --> 01:17:06,947
You know, I'm in
the middle of this, uh...

1071
01:17:07,970 --> 01:17:11,523
investigation. The NTSB
is investigating the crash.

1072
01:17:11,553 --> 01:17:13,203
Yeah, I'm gonna talk to ‘em next week.

1073
01:17:13,221 --> 01:17:17,007
Yeah. Well, I-I-
I'm a little nervous because...

1074
01:17:18,214 --> 01:17:21,964
I was out the night
before the crash, the diner-

1075
01:17:21,994 --> 01:17:22,931
Yeah. With Katerina.

1076
01:17:22,973 --> 01:17:27,528
Right. And I had two glasses of wine,
and they're probably gonna ask you

1077
01:17:27,637 --> 01:17:30,189
about my condition that morning, or

1078
01:17:30,190 --> 01:17:32,990
whether you thought
I had anything to drink.

1079
01:17:36,536 --> 01:17:37,983
What?

1080
01:17:41,120 --> 01:17:43,063
I've known you eleven years, Whip.

1081
01:17:43,149 --> 01:17:44,004
Right.

1082
01:17:44,286 --> 01:17:48,718
You want to tell me that you and Trina went
to diner and you had two glasses of wine,

1083
01:17:48,727 --> 01:17:50,771
sounds like a nice restaurant,
now which one was it?

1084
01:17:50,825 --> 01:17:53,705
You have to say that it was
an ordinary day, Margaret.

1085
01:17:53,726 --> 01:17:57,275
I mean, it was an ordinary day.
You know me, I was in shape to fly.

1086
01:18:02,178 --> 01:18:04,285
You have a problem saying that?

1087
01:18:06,022 --> 01:18:08,809
It's a lie, Whip... It's a lie.

1088
01:18:08,947 --> 01:18:11,453
Trina told me you two
hadn't been to sleep.

1089
01:18:11,493 --> 01:18:14,045
Well, the... my lack of
sleep had nothing to do

1090
01:18:14,062 --> 01:18:16,178
with that plane falling apart, Margaret.

1091
01:18:16,574 --> 01:18:19,046
I'm just trying to get these things
straight, now you think any other pilot

1092
01:18:19,047 --> 01:18:21,001
could have landed that plane
and saved more lives?

1093
01:18:21,014 --> 01:18:21,936
I didn't say that, Whip,

1094
01:18:21,952 --> 01:18:23,072
– Alright, then...
– I can't imagine another pilot...

1095
01:18:23,093 --> 01:18:27,458
Imagine your son Trevor in this
church looking at you in that box,

1096
01:18:27,467 --> 01:18:30,746
or my son visiting me in prison,
and for the next forty years-

1097
01:18:30,757 --> 01:18:33,044
Please, Whip, enough.
Enough, Whip.

1098
01:18:33,069 --> 01:18:35,253
Don't you think we've all had enough?

1099
01:18:43,058 --> 01:18:44,821
I need this, Margaret.

1100
01:18:52,338 --> 01:18:54,297
What d'you want me to say?

1101
01:18:56,241 --> 01:18:57,668
I just...

1102
01:18:59,242 --> 01:19:00,919
It was an ordinary day.

1103
01:19:03,576 --> 01:19:07,105
The storm... after we took off...

1104
01:19:07,416 --> 01:19:09,409
was very severe.

1105
01:19:10,671 --> 01:19:13,668
But as far as the flight crew
is concerned, it was a...

1106
01:19:14,591 --> 01:19:16,691
a perfectly ordinary day.

1107
01:19:43,145 --> 01:19:44,494
You okay?

1108
01:19:44,562 --> 01:19:46,213
Uh yeah, yeah.

1109
01:19:47,012 --> 01:19:48,425
So?

1110
01:19:48,627 --> 01:19:51,327
Well, I got through the first day,
they didn-t fire me,

1111
01:19:51,390 --> 01:19:52,790
so...
– Then?

1112
01:19:53,010 --> 01:19:55,945
And they need someone to work
the night shift, and they asked me,

1113
01:19:56,052 --> 01:19:58,125
it’s time and a half so...

1114
01:19:59,731 --> 01:20:01,541
I got a vest...

1115
01:20:02,354 --> 01:20:04,588
and a... well, name tag,
I got you something.

1116
01:20:04,688 --> 01:20:06,508
– Yeah?
– Yeah.

1117
01:20:06,602 --> 01:20:08,002
Thank you.

1118
01:20:10,754 --> 01:20:11,737
– It is a P-51 mustang.

1119
01:20:11,738 --> 01:20:13,174
– It is a P-51 mustang.
– I know it's a P-51.

1120
01:20:13,205 --> 01:20:13,902
– Your daddy fly one of these?

1121
01:20:13,903 --> 01:20:14,915
– Your daddy fly one of these?
– Yes, he did.

1122
01:20:16,303 --> 01:20:18,246
It cause for celebration.

1123
01:20:18,272 --> 01:20:21,488
Oh well, I promised Sheila that
I'd go to this AA meeting now.

1124
01:20:21,932 --> 01:20:23,755
She's my sponsor, the one
that's been helping me,

1125
01:20:23,758 --> 01:20:25,185
can we celebrate tomorrow night?

1126
01:20:26,171 --> 01:20:27,270
Yeah.

1127
01:20:28,011 --> 01:20:29,856
Yeah sure, why not?

1128
01:20:33,667 --> 01:20:36,621
You know, you can come with me...
to the meeting.

1129
01:20:36,862 --> 01:20:39,494
It's an open meeting,
meaning that anyone can come.

1130
01:20:40,396 --> 01:20:41,437
Even you!

1131
01:20:43,246 --> 01:20:45,603
So the cops got a flashlight on
me behind the dumpster, I'm...

1132
01:20:45,622 --> 01:20:47,321
I'm wearing nothing but
my girlfriend’s panties,

1133
01:20:47,335 --> 01:20:49,395
I got vomitin' on the
front of my chest,

1134
01:20:50,247 --> 01:20:54,028
and I say to him:
"Uh... I might have had two beers."

1135
01:21:01,089 --> 01:21:03,608
So that's why I get the nickname:
"Two-Beer Barry".

1136
01:21:06,495 --> 01:21:09,164
I'm getting the, uh... the
stink eye from Ed over here,

1137
01:21:09,180 --> 01:21:10,766
have I gone off format?

1138
01:21:12,064 --> 01:21:14,389
My name is Barry and I'm an alcoholic.

1139
01:21:14,515 --> 01:21:15,796
Hi Barry.

1140
01:21:15,855 --> 01:21:18,938
Are there any other alcoholics present?

1141
01:21:24,248 --> 01:21:27,338
I always love a meeting where
we all have to identify the tab,

1142
01:21:27,347 --> 01:21:30,836
because it forces me to be
honest about who I really am.

1143
01:21:32,473 --> 01:21:37,026
And I never told the truth out there,
obviously I lied about everything.

1144
01:21:38,527 --> 01:21:41,566
I was told in these rooms that
I was never sober if I kept lying,

1145
01:21:41,574 --> 01:21:43,319
but that's what I was best at.

1146
01:21:46,026 --> 01:21:48,313
If I knew anything in this
world, it was how to lie,

1147
01:21:48,320 --> 01:21:51,639
especially about my drinking.
But...

1148
01:21:51,849 --> 01:21:55,072
whatever, I would lie about
the name of my dog,

1149
01:21:55,073 --> 01:21:58,336
I'd lie about the color of my
own hair right to your face.

1150
01:22:00,012 --> 01:22:05,236
My whole life just became... a series
of lies, strung together by me,

1151
01:22:05,257 --> 01:22:06,637
I gotta go.

1152
01:22:07,043 --> 01:22:08,163
Sure.

1153
01:22:08,636 --> 01:22:11,274
Oh yeah, Sheila, she can
give me a ride, does she?

1154
01:22:11,953 --> 01:22:13,073
I'm Sheila.

1155
01:22:33,356 --> 01:22:37,049
<i>I’ve been speaking to some people who
are very close to the NTSB investigation,</i>

1156
01:22:37,059 --> 01:22:40,338
<i>and they are confident they will
soon be able to determine exactly</i>

1157
01:22:40,340 --> 01:22:41,939
<i>what brought this plane down.</i>

1158
01:22:41,946 --> 01:22:44,515
<i>I'm sure they will, sir.</i>

1159
01:22:44,516 --> 01:22:47,536
<i>Well, Ken and Vicki Evans,
thank you very much.</i>

1160
01:22:47,552 --> 01:22:50,433
<i>Thank you sir. God bless you.</i>

1161
01:23:02,735 --> 01:23:04,991
How you feeling, Ken?

1162
01:23:06,754 --> 01:23:08,463
I'm happy to be alive.

1163
01:23:08,545 --> 01:23:10,735
Blessed... to be alive.

1164
01:23:13,071 --> 01:23:16,021
I'm Captain Whitaker, I was...
flying the-

1165
01:23:16,032 --> 01:23:17,418
I know who you are.

1166
01:23:18,867 --> 01:23:22,177
Excuse me. Can you give us
some privacy, please?

1167
01:23:22,248 --> 01:23:23,761
Thank you.

1168
01:23:35,178 --> 01:23:37,285
So, uh...

1169
01:23:37,543 --> 01:23:39,363
How long have you been out of the...

1170
01:23:39,726 --> 01:23:41,566
– Out of the coma?
– Yeah.

1171
01:23:42,383 --> 01:23:43,778
Two days.

1172
01:23:48,526 --> 01:23:52,148
Well, uh, look, I don't wanna...
disrupt your whole day,

1173
01:23:53,079 --> 01:23:57,920
God knows you've been...
swamped by interviews, and...

1174
01:23:59,475 --> 01:24:02,410
You looked brillant on the CNN,
by the way.

1175
01:24:02,980 --> 01:24:06,643
Both my legs are crushed.
My pelvis is snapped.

1176
01:24:07,543 --> 01:24:12,046
I'll probably never walk again.
Al least not without a walker or braces.

1177
01:24:13,132 --> 01:24:15,333
I'll certainly never fly again.

1178
01:24:16,542 --> 01:24:18,138
I'm very sorry, Ken.

1179
01:24:18,864 --> 01:24:22,096
That plane was doomed
the moment you sat in that cockpit.

1180
01:24:22,848 --> 01:24:25,890
You reeked like gin or somethin’.

1181
01:24:26,315 --> 01:24:29,115
I called Vicki from the plane
before we took off.

1182
01:24:30,532 --> 01:24:33,044
That’s when the rain kicked up.

1183
01:24:34,418 --> 01:24:38,470
Well, I don’t know how much
you remember, but...

1184
01:24:39,094 --> 01:24:40,662
that plane...

1185
01:24:41,131 --> 01:24:42,967
it started falling apart, Ken.

1186
01:24:43,117 --> 01:24:45,810
I remember everything until the crash.

1187
01:24:46,095 --> 01:24:47,700
I know what went wrong.

1188
01:24:50,075 --> 01:24:53,088
What are you trying to say, Ken?
What do we talk?

1189
01:24:54,023 --> 01:24:57,270
I do not know, Captain Whitaker.
What are we talking about?

1190
01:24:57,298 --> 01:25:00,543
I'm just trying to get a sense from you...

1191
01:25:01,544 --> 01:25:05,346
about what you thought caused the crash.

1192
01:25:06,645 --> 01:25:10,642
Was it the fact that you got on the
plane still drunk from the night before?

1193
01:25:12,490 --> 01:25:16,519
The NTSB is coming back tomorrow
to finish taking a deposition from me...

1194
01:25:17,725 --> 01:25:19,574
about the events on that flight.

1195
01:25:20,006 --> 01:25:24,144
Do you think you... be alive
if I wasn't flying that plane?

